//! Room layouts: the wall network generated by the rooms of one plane.
//!
//! A layout owns the arrangement of its rooms and generates ONE wall
//! network from it, so a boundary shared by two rooms is one wall and T
//! and X junctions are exact.
//!
//! **Effective regions.** Rooms are ranked by precedence (higher first;
//! ties by entity id). A room's effective region is its boundary minus
//! the union of the boundaries of the rooms ranked above it, so effective
//! regions never overlap. A region can be empty or in several pieces
//! ([`RegionStatus`]).
//!
//! **Wall graph.** The edges of all effective regions, split at every
//! vertex that lies on them and deduplicated: a segment shared by two
//! regions is one wall. Each segment knows the original room edges that
//! cover it (collinear overlap). A segment is hidden (no wall) when ANY
//! covering room edge is hidden.
//!
//! **Footprint.** Walls are centered on the visible segments,
//! `thickness_m` wide: one rectangle per segment, ending square at both
//! nodes, plus at every node with two or more walls a kite that fills
//! each outside corner (a gap between consecutive walls wider than a
//! half turn) up to the miter point of the two wall faces, or a bevel
//! when the miter would reach farther than [`MITER_LIMIT`] half
//! thicknesses. Inside corners, T and X junctions need no filler: the
//! rectangles meet exactly. A wall end at a node with no other wall is
//! square. The union of all pieces is the footprint.
//!
//! **Openings** are anchored to a room edge: `offset_m` along it from its
//! start to the opening's left edge. An opening must lie in one
//! [`opening_span`] of its edge: the part of the edge covered by visible
//! wall, clear of the other walls at its junctions. A through opening
//! cuts the whole thickness; a niche is `depth_m` deep from the face on
//! the room's side (the left of the room edge, its interior). An opening
//! that does not fit is left out and reported ([`LayoutIssue`]).
//!
//! **Height layers.** The wall rises from the plane to the top reference
//! H (as for wall runs). Heights 0, H, and every opening's bottom and
//! top split it into layers; each layer is the footprint minus the
//! openings active in it, as prisms.

